<h2>The background</h2>
<p>A thirteen-year-old girl went missing from her home in March 2021 and was traced twelve days later in the company of the appellant — her mother&rsquo;s &ldquo;dharam bhai&rdquo;, who lived with the family. Her age was proved through school records. She alleged repeated sexual assault, beating and threats. The trial court convicted the appellant and imposed twenty years&rsquo; rigorous imprisonment under Section 6 POCSO with concurrent sentences on the IPC counts and victim compensation of Rs 10,50,000.</p>

<h2>The sole-testimony standard</h2>
<p>The appeal pressed inconsistencies and investigation defects: the delay in the missing report, a date discrepancy in a counselling record, the delayed Section 164 statement, seizure-memo mismatches, the absence of CCTV footage, a sister not examined, a nil-injury MLC, and a false-implication theory. The court&rsquo;s answer came from the settled standard:</p>
<div class="note">
<p><strong>From the judgment, quoting the Supreme Court:</strong> &ldquo;The conviction can be sustained on the sole testimony of the prosecutrix, if it inspires confidence.&rdquo; On the record, the court found the victim&rsquo;s testimony &ldquo;sufficiently cogent&rdquo;.</p>
</div>
<p>Her account was consistent across the Section 161 statement, the Section 164 statement and her deposition. <em>Nirmal Premkumar</em> — where the Supreme Court declined to act on shaky sole testimony — was distinguished on its facts; <em>Manga Singh</em> and <em>Phool Singh</em> supplied the governing rule. Investigative lapses, the court held, do not discard otherwise reliable substantive evidence.</p>

<h2>Why the trust element mattered</h2>
<p>The aggravating clauses did independent work. Section 5(l) was met by the repetition of the assaults; Section 5(n) by the appellant&rsquo;s position — a person living in the same household, treated as family. The provision recognises that the household offender both enjoys access and commands silence, and the twenty-year minimum that follows under Section 6 reflects that legislative judgment. Given the gravity, the court declined to interfere with sentence, maintaining also the compensation and the set-off for custody undergone.</p>

<h2>What the decision means in practice</h2>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><strong>For the defence.</strong> A catalogue of investigative lapses is not a defence case. Appellate courts ask one question first: does the victim&rsquo;s core account hold? If it does, peripheral defects rarely move the needle — the realistic targets are material contradictions in the victim&rsquo;s own narrative.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>For prosecutors.</strong> Consistency across the Section 161 and 164 statements and the deposition is the backbone of a sole-testimony conviction. Preserving that chain, and proving age cleanly through school records, decided this appeal.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>On sentencing.</strong> For offences after the 2019 amendment, Section 6 carries a twenty-year minimum. Mitigation arguments that ignore the statutory floor are wasted; the realistic sentencing questions concern offences pre-dating the amendment.</div>
</div>

<h2>The wider frame</h2>
<p>The &ldquo;dharam bhai&rdquo; of this case joins a long, grim catalogue of household figures in POCSO dockets — uncles, tenants, family friends — whose access flows from trust. The statute&rsquo;s aggravation scheme, and the courts&rsquo; steady application of the sole-testimony standard, together answer the evidentiary reality of such cases: they happen behind closed doors, and the child&rsquo;s word, if it withstands scrutiny, is the case. This judgment is a current and complete illustration of that framework in operation in the Delhi High Court.</p>
